  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>We know that angle of deviation depend upon the angle of incidence. If we determine experimentally the angle of deviation corresponding to different angles of incidence then and then plot <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi></math> (on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#948;</mi></math> (on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi></math>-axis), we get a curve as shown in figure.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/0e4d8c2a-73dd-4fd8-baf3-0675c44f6e69-image.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 268px;\" /></p><p>Clearly, if angle of incidence is gradually increased from a small value, the angle of deviation first decreases, becomes minimum for a particular angle of incidence and then begins to increase.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
